              "text": "**Medi-Cal** is California\u2019s Medicaid program, which provides free or low-cost health coverage for eligible children and adults with limited income and resources. \n\nThe Medi-Cal landscape has underwent **major legal and programmatic changes**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Major Medi-Cal Changes\nIn response to the California state budget and federal legislation (such as H.R. 1), several critical changes went into effect:\n\n*   **Reinstatement of Asset Limits (As of January 1, 2026):** For several years, California had eliminated asset testing. However, **asset limits have been reinstated** for individuals whose eligibility is based on age (65+), disability, or long-term care needs (non-MAGI Medi-Cal). \n    *   **The Limits:** Individual countable assets are limited to **$130,000**, and **$195,000** for married couples (plus $65,000 for each additional family member up to 10). \n    *   *Note:* Primary homes, one vehicle, and personal belongings generally do not count toward this limit.\n*   **Immigration-Related Enrollment Freeze (As of January 1, 2026):** \n    *   **New Applicants:** New enrollments for full-scope Medi-Cal are frozen for undocumented adults (ages 19 and older) who do not have satisfactory immigration status. New applicants in this category will only qualify for Emergency or pregnancy-related Medi-Cal.\n    *   **Current Members:** If you were already enrolled in full-scope Medi-Cal before January 1, 2026, you will keep your full coverage as long as you complete your annual renewals and continue to meet income requirements.\n*   **Loss of Adult Dental Benefits (July 1, 2026):** Dental benefits will no longer be covered for undocumented adults ages 19 and older. (Children under 19 and pregnant individuals are exempt and will keep dental coverage).\n*   **Upcoming Work Requirements (Starting January 1, 2027):** Under federal law (H.R. 1), working-age adults (ages 19\u201364) enrolled in Medi-Cal will need to meet work, school, or volunteer requirements (generally 80 hours a month) to keep coverage, unless they qualify for an exemption (such as caring for a child under 14, having a disability, or being medically frail).\n\n---\n\n### 2. General Eligibility Rules\nMedi-Cal eligibility is primarily based on your household size and income:\n\n*   **Income Limits:** For most adults, families, and children, eligibility is determined by Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI). Generally, individuals with an income up to **138% of the Federal Poverty Level (FPL)** qualify. \n*   **Who Qualifies:**\n    *   Low-income adults and families\n    *   Children and pregnant individuals\n    *   Seniors (65 and older)\n    *   People with disabilities\n    *   Former foster youth (up to age 26)\n\n---\n\n### 3. What Does Medi-Cal Cover?\nFor those with full-scope coverage, Medi-Cal covers \"Essential Health Benefits,\" which include:\n*   Doctor visits and preventative care\n*   Emergency services and hospitalization\n*   Maternity, newborn, and pediatric care\n*   Mental health and substance use disorder services\n*   Prescription drugs\n*   Rehabilitative services and devices\n*   Laboratory services\n*   Vision exams and glasses\n*   Long-term care and nursing home care (subject to the asset limits mentioned above)\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Apply or Renew Coverage\nIf you need to apply for the first time, or if you received a yellow renewal packet in the mail (which you must complete annually to avoid losing coverage), you can do so in several ways:\n\n1.  **Online:** Apply or renew via [BenefitsCal.com](https://benefitscal.com/) or [Covered California](https://www.coveredca.com/).\n2.  **By Phone:** Call your local county social services office or the Covered California helpline.\n3.  **In Person:** Visit your county\u2019s Department of Social Services office.\n4.  **By Mail:** Mail a completed paper application to your county office.",
